  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Nicknamed "Tire-Poil" ("hair-puller") because of the near-constant wind that tousles walkers' hair, this coastal trail follows the south-eastern tip of Cap d'Antibes. The route alternates rocky stretches right at the water's edge, steps cut into the rock and views of the lavish properties lining the cape, with Antibes and Juan-les-Pins as a backdrop.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>According to the Antibes Juan-les-Pins tourist office, the trail is about 5 km and takes about 2 hours; it is fitted with paths and steps in the steep areas, and is easy as long as you wear good shoes. The start is from the large free Garoupe car park (avenue André Sella). Note: a stretch of about 50 m below Villa Eilenroc is closed until May 2027 to secure the cliff, and the trail is closed in rain or strong wind. This part of the cape is part of the Natura 2000 site "Baie et cap d'Antibes – îles de Lérins". Neither the town nor the tourist office publishes a precise dog rule for this trail: to be safe, keep your dog on a leash, pick up after it and bring water, as there is little shade over a good part of the route.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: avoid peak hours in summer — the trail is narrow in places and hard to pass with a dog on a leash when it's busy.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The Plage de la Garoupe, right by the starting point, and the Garoupe lighthouse offer another view over the bay and the Esterel massif on a clear day.</p>
